WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.290 --> 00:00:05.720
MIT is known for developing a lot of impressive
technology.

00:00:05.720 --> 00:00:11.710
But hidden in the kitchen of MIT’s Media
Lab is, perhaps,

00:00:11.710 --> 00:00:16.040
my favorite MIT invention: the FoodCam.

00:00:16.040 --> 00:00:21.399
Okay, so it may not look like much but it’s
actually quite brilliant.

00:00:21.399 --> 00:00:26.820
Let’s say you have some leftover food.

00:00:26.820 --> 00:00:29.630
You put it under the camera and you hit the
button.

00:00:29.630 --> 00:00:33.590
FoodCam posts a photo to Twitter, Slack, and
a mailing list.

00:00:33.590 --> 00:00:36.460
All with a simple message: Come and get it!

00:00:36.460 --> 00:00:38.560
It looks like a pretty good box of donuts.

00:00:38.560 --> 00:00:39.560
Yes.

00:00:39.560 --> 00:00:40.560
It looks yummy under FoodCam.

00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:41.560
It does.

00:00:41.560 --> 00:00:42.760
Getting the food can actually be pretty competitive.

00:00:42.760 --> 00:00:46.430
By the time we got here, just 30 seconds after
it was placed,

00:00:46.430 --> 00:00:49.190
the whole building had swarmed and all the
pizza was gone.

00:00:49.190 --> 00:00:51.350
There’s a mad rush of people that come from,
like,

00:00:51.350 --> 00:00:53.560
every entryway in here to get the pizza.

00:00:53.560 --> 00:00:55.540
So you got to kind of move pretty quickly.

00:00:55.540 --> 00:00:58.340
Yeah, it’s a game —&nbsp;it’s like the Hunger
Games.

00:00:58.340 --> 00:00:59.340
Where...

00:00:59.340 --> 00:01:02.750
Will and Jon invented the FoodCam all the
way back in 1999.

00:01:02.750 --> 00:01:04.960
This was before Facebook.

00:01:04.960 --> 00:01:05.960
Before Gmail.

00:01:05.960 --> 00:01:08.390
Before social media as we know it.

00:01:08.390 --> 00:01:11.909
The idea came from a building-wide leftovers
problem.

00:01:11.909 --> 00:01:16.750
And in some ways, this simple invention gets
at the big problem of food waste.

00:01:16.750 --> 00:01:21.190
I mean that's sort of the serious part of
what you have done, really, right?

00:01:21.190 --> 00:01:25.479
There is no doubt that this completely helped
reduce food waste at the lab.

00:01:25.479 --> 00:01:30.140
Almost all of the catering people know that
if they have spare food from their event,

00:01:30.140 --> 00:01:33.900
they can just hit the button and people will
consume that food.

00:01:33.900 --> 00:01:37.590
And those are not even Media Lab events that
are now fueling the FoodCam.

00:01:37.590 --> 00:01:40.900
When we picture the stuff that’s hurting
our planet, what do we think of?

00:01:40.900 --> 00:01:44.369
We think of, like, smokestacks, cars, oil
spills.

00:01:44.369 --> 00:01:48.150
We don’t really think about all the food
we throw away.

00:01:48.150 --> 00:01:53.070
In the US, roughly 40% of the food we produce
never gets eaten.

00:01:53.070 --> 00:01:58.090
That's over 365 million pounds of food each
day.

00:01:58.090 --> 00:02:01.060
While that’s happening, about one in eight
Americans

00:02:01.060 --> 00:02:04.189
still don’t have a steady supply of food
to their tables.

00:02:04.189 --> 00:02:08.450
And all of this wasted food is a huge contributor
to climate change.

00:02:08.450 --> 00:02:10.399
If global food waste were a country,

00:02:10.399 --> 00:02:14.220
it would be the third largest emitter of greenhouse
gases,

00:02:14.220 --> 00:02:17.049
just behind China and the United States.

00:02:17.049 --> 00:02:20.870
So it really is an enormous problem and one
of the easiest ways

00:02:20.870 --> 00:02:22.790
to address climate change.

00:02:22.790 --> 00:02:25.579
It takes a ton of resources to produce food.

00:02:25.579 --> 00:02:26.579
On top of that,

00:02:26.579 --> 00:02:31.769
you have all of the energy it takes to keep
it cold and transport it around the country.

00:02:31.769 --> 00:02:34.519
And when food decomposes, it isn’t just
stinky.

00:02:34.519 --> 00:02:37.290
It releases potent greenhouse gases.

00:02:37.290 --> 00:02:38.290
Basically,

00:02:38.290 --> 00:02:43.420
we’re trashing our planet to grow food that
no one eats.But here’s the thing: No one

00:02:43.420 --> 00:02:45.389
actually likes wasting food.

00:02:45.389 --> 00:02:49.170
It’s just something that we haven’t been
paying much attention to.

00:02:49.170 --> 00:02:52.950
Of all of the challenging problems out there,
reducing the amount of food

00:02:52.950 --> 00:02:55.779
we're wasting is one of the easiest.

00:02:55.779 --> 00:03:00.559
In the US, consumers collectively make up
the largest portion of food waste.

00:03:00.559 --> 00:03:06.730
A family of four spends about $1,500 on food
that they never eat.

00:03:06.730 --> 00:03:12.220
Meat is less as a percentage of what we buy
but when you consider it in particular,

00:03:12.220 --> 00:03:16.010
as a greenhouse gas intensive product, meat
waste actually

00:03:16.010 --> 00:03:18.890
has the highest greenhouse gas impact.

00:03:18.890 --> 00:03:24.079
And you don't have to be an expert to understand
why food is going to waste in our homes.

00:03:24.079 --> 00:03:26.120
We’re all busy and on the go.

00:03:26.120 --> 00:03:27.930
Sometimes I buy food without thinking,

00:03:27.930 --> 00:03:29.839
“Do I really need that?”

00:03:29.839 --> 00:03:33.370
There's even been a little bit of research
to show that once something goes

00:03:33.370 --> 00:03:37.959
in the refrigerator it's actually worth less
to us than before.

00:03:37.959 --> 00:03:38.959
&nbsp;

00:03:38.959 --> 00:03:40.769
Researchers asked people how they would feel
if they got home

00:03:40.769 --> 00:03:44.249
from the grocery store and dropped a carton
of eggs.

00:03:44.249 --> 00:03:47.669
And then they asked, well if your eggs sat
in your refrigerator for six weeks and then

00:03:47.669 --> 00:03:49.769
you didn't use them, how would you feel about
that?

00:03:49.769 --> 00:03:51.699
And people felt a lot less remorse.

00:03:51.699 --> 00:03:54.559
I think a lot of the waste in our society
does

00:03:54.559 --> 00:03:59.779
come down to choice and wanting to have the
option to eat something at any time,

00:03:59.779 --> 00:04:00.999
whether or not we use it.

00:04:00.999 --> 00:04:06.439
Part of the reason we over-buy food is that
we’ve got tons of space to store it in.

00:04:06.439 --> 00:04:10.489
Refrigerators have grown about 15% since the
1970s.

00:04:10.489 --> 00:04:16.479
One of the things we found in our research
is that people are uncomfortable with

00:04:16.479 --> 00:04:18.690
white space when it comes to food.

00:04:18.690 --> 00:04:21.920
So we love it in buildings, or in design,

00:04:21.920 --> 00:04:26.440
but when it comes to food, we do not want
to see empty space in our refrigerators,

00:04:26.440 --> 00:04:29.860
on our plates, and so I really believe that
in some

00:04:29.860 --> 00:04:32.100
subliminal way we're just filling everything.

00:04:32.100 --> 00:04:37.240
And if we had smaller refrigerators, that
let us see everything that was in there,

00:04:37.240 --> 00:04:39.710
that in itself would lead to quite a bit less
waste in our homes.

00:04:39.710 --> 00:04:43.280
And it isn’t just our refrigerators that
have gotten bigger.

00:04:43.280 --> 00:04:48.370
The average dinner plate has grown by 36%
since 1960.

00:04:48.370 --> 00:04:51.390
When you have a big plate, you tend to put
a lot of food on it —&nbsp;

00:04:51.390 --> 00:04:53.350
whether or not you can eat it all.

00:04:53.350 --> 00:04:58.100
This is something Jill Horst noticed at UC
Santa Barbara.

00:04:58.100 --> 00:05:02.630
You have a tray that's 14-by-18 inches and
you feel you need to load it up with food.

00:05:02.630 --> 00:05:07.340
You would see students that had four glasses:
water, juice, soda, milk —

00:05:07.340 --> 00:05:11.250
and you'd go to the tray return and they would
still be full.

00:05:11.250 --> 00:05:14.780
In 2009, the dining halls stopped using trays.

00:05:14.780 --> 00:05:19.180
Students can take as much food as they want,
but there isn’t a tray to pile it onto.

00:05:19.180 --> 00:05:22.680
The food waste per person, per tray, reduced
by 50 percent.

00:05:22.680 --> 00:05:24.210
I mean so that was huge.

00:05:24.210 --> 00:05:28.380
Let’s say that the average student wastes
six ounces of food per meal.

00:05:28.380 --> 00:05:33.560
That may not seem like a lot —&nbsp;but UC Santa
Barbara serves 13,000 meals per day.

00:05:33.560 --> 00:05:37.160
So that’s nearly 5,000 pounds of wasted
food.

00:05:37.160 --> 00:05:41.630
It's like throwing 350 Thanksgiving turkeys
into the garbage every single day.

00:05:41.630 --> 00:05:46.490
And when you take the trays away and it becomes
three ounces, that's a significant impact

00:05:46.490 --> 00:05:49.900
to help with not only the food waste, but
food cost.

00:05:49.900 --> 00:05:54.840
So, it turns out that something very small
— like removing a tray or changing the

00:05:54.840 --> 00:05:59.440
size of a plate — can have this profound
impact on our behavior.

00:05:59.440 --> 00:06:03.840
And it doesn’t take much effort, because
the effect is subliminal.

00:06:03.840 --> 00:06:08.730
The other thing they’re paying attention
to at UC Santa Barbara is portion size.

00:06:08.730 --> 00:06:12.351
Each plate is portioned one portion for a
student.

00:06:12.351 --> 00:06:14.120
They can take as many portions as they like,

00:06:14.120 --> 00:06:18.210
but we are actually plating the right size,
the right amount that we should be eating.

00:06:18.210 --> 00:06:22.310
We’ve gotten used to these gigantic portion
sizes at restaurants.

00:06:22.310 --> 00:06:27.440
And in a subtle way, it encourages us to overeat
and throw away a lot of food.

00:06:27.440 --> 00:06:31.420
If you look around, there’s not a whole
lot of food waste on the plates

00:06:31.420 --> 00:06:32.420
because of the proper portioning.

00:06:32.420 --> 00:06:34.310
I mean that’s somebody’s meal.

00:06:34.310 --> 00:06:37.900
That’s all they have left.

00:06:37.900 --> 00:06:39.870
None of us are perfect.

00:06:39.870 --> 00:06:42.650
Wasting less food isn’t just going to happen
overnight.

00:06:42.650 --> 00:06:47.030
But just having it on our radar can really
help us waste a lot less.

00:06:47.030 --> 00:06:48.030
&nbsp;

00:06:48.030 --> 00:06:50.920
And if we do have extra food, then let’s
at least try to get it to people who could

00:06:50.920 --> 00:06:51.920
use it.

00:06:51.920 --> 00:06:55.620
There is so much high-quality surplus that's
wasted,

00:06:55.620 --> 00:06:58.420
that just needs to find the people that need
it the most.

00:06:58.420 --> 00:07:03.480
Komal is the founder of Copia, a startup that’s
trying to recover all of this perfectly good

00:07:03.480 --> 00:07:04.480
food.

00:07:04.480 --> 00:07:08.200
If you imagine the world's largest football
stadium filled to its absolute brim

00:07:08.200 --> 00:07:10.889
that's how much food goes wasted every single
day in America —

00:07:10.889 --> 00:07:14.520
and I'm not talking about last night's pad
thai or this morning's half-eaten pastries,

00:07:14.520 --> 00:07:17.889
but untouched, uneaten, perfectly edible food.

00:07:17.889 --> 00:07:18.889
&nbsp;

00:07:18.889 --> 00:07:20.860
So we don’t need to purchase or make more
food.

00:07:20.860 --> 00:07:24.380
We just need to figure out how to get it to
the people who need it.

00:07:24.380 --> 00:07:27.430
MIT’s FoodCam is great at recovering food.

00:07:27.430 --> 00:07:32.830
But when you start scaling this up from one
building to an entire city or an entire country,

00:07:32.830 --> 00:07:34.440
it becomes much more difficult.

00:07:34.440 --> 00:07:39.540
Let’s say you’re a small company and have
200 sandwiches left over from an event.

00:07:39.540 --> 00:07:44.170
That’s a lot of food — but it takes time
and effort to figure out how and where to

00:07:44.170 --> 00:07:45.170
donate it.

00:07:45.170 --> 00:07:47.250
Most people really don’t want to deal with
all this.

00:07:47.250 --> 00:07:49.870
It shouldn’t be this hard to do a good thing.

00:07:49.870 --> 00:07:53.260
Like, how cool would it be if people who have
food could say, hey, we have food,

00:07:53.260 --> 00:07:55.090
and people who need food could say, hey we
need food,

00:07:55.090 --> 00:07:57.590
and we could connect these two people and
clear the marketplace?

00:07:57.590 --> 00:08:01.280
So Komal is trying to make food donation easy
and intuitive.

00:08:01.280 --> 00:08:04.690
If you have some food, you type your info
into the Copia app.

00:08:04.690 --> 00:08:10.050
A driver will then come pick up your food
and deliver it to shelters that need it.

00:08:10.050 --> 00:08:14.750
And during big events, like Super Bowl 50,
there’s a ton of extra food.

00:08:14.750 --> 00:08:17.780
The issue is that it has a short shelf life.

00:08:17.780 --> 00:08:21.440
Imagine four 16-foot refrigerated trucks filled
to their absolute brim —

00:08:21.440 --> 00:08:22.810
that's how much food we recovered.

00:08:22.810 --> 00:08:26.540
We fed 23,000 people in two days.

00:08:26.540 --> 00:08:27.570
Nobody slept.

00:08:27.570 --> 00:08:29.840
And it's not you know hot dogs and popcorn.

00:08:29.840 --> 00:08:34.339
It was lobster rolls and pulled pork sandwiches
and $300 cheeses.

00:08:34.339 --> 00:08:35.339
High-quality food.

00:08:35.339 --> 00:08:39.389
If we can get food that would otherwise be
wasted to people who need it,

00:08:39.389 --> 00:08:43.519
we’re not only fighting hunger, but we’re
actually slowing global warming.

00:08:43.519 --> 00:08:45.779
It really is a win-win.

00:08:45.779 --> 00:08:48.860
And Komal doesn’t want to solve hunger in
just California.

00:08:48.860 --> 00:08:51.180
She wants to solve world hunger — period.

00:08:51.180 --> 00:08:53.949
It's not about optimism or pessimism.

00:08:53.949 --> 00:08:57.320
I think it's just that we're hell-bent on
making it happen.

00:08:57.320 --> 00:08:58.939
This isn't going to be an overnight thing.

00:08:58.939 --> 00:08:59.970
It's got to be policy change.

00:08:59.970 --> 00:09:01.079
It's going to be other entrepreneurs.

00:09:01.079 --> 00:09:05.509
It's going to be really big companies and
institutions also taking a stand

00:09:05.509 --> 00:09:06.630
and saying that you know what?

00:09:06.630 --> 00:09:10.779
We don't tolerate perfectly great food being
wasted.

00:09:10.779 --> 00:09:14.149
Look, no one likes throwing out food.

00:09:14.149 --> 00:09:16.860
So we made a simple guide to help you waste
less.

00:09:16.860 --> 00:09:20.670
To find out more go to climate.universityofcalifornia.edu.

